1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:06,685
Welcome to chapter fourteen of my study series on Build Your First LLM by Hasan Degismez.

2
00:00:06,685 --> 00:00:09,072
Last chapter changed the weights.

3
00:00:09,072 --> 00:00:21,871
Today we change only the words we send: temperature and top p, few shot, chain of thought, delimiters, structured output, prompt injection, and how to evaluate prompts properly.

4
00:00:21,871 --> 00:00:32,862
I ran the notebook, and then ran the chapter's prompts on a real Llama 3.2 3B model on my own machine, so you'll see actual scores rather than promises.

5
00:00:33,712 --> 00:00:36,197
Chapter thirteen changed weights.

6
00:00:36,197 --> 00:00:38,532
Today we only change the input.

7
00:00:38,532 --> 00:00:41,619
Chapter fifteen adds knowledge and tools.

8
00:00:41,619 --> 00:00:50,053
The notebook uses MiniGPT for the sampling demos and Ollama with Llama 3.2 for the parts that need a real model.

9
00:00:50,903 --> 00:00:51,570
The plan.

10
00:00:51,570 --> 00:00:53,273
The completion mindset.

11
00:00:53,273 --> 00:00:53,717
Roles.

12
00:00:53,717 --> 00:00:55,346
Temperature and top p.

13
00:00:55,346 --> 00:00:57,197
The core prompt patterns.

14
00:00:57,197 --> 00:00:59,418
Delimiters and output formats.

15
00:00:59,418 --> 00:01:01,121
Injection and defences.

16
00:01:01,121 --> 00:01:04,749
And evaluation, with my scores from a real model.

17
00:01:05,599 --> 00:01:06,305
Key terms.

18
00:01:06,305 --> 00:01:09,058
The system message sets role and rules.

19
00:01:09,058 --> 00:01:11,317
Few shot means showing examples.

20
00:01:11,317 --> 00:01:14,281
Chain of thought asks for reasoning first.

21
00:01:14,281 --> 00:01:18,023
Prompt injection is user text trying to override you.

22
00:01:18,023 --> 00:01:21,552
Temperature sharpens or flattens the distribution.

23
00:01:21,552 --> 00:01:27,764
And top p samples from the smallest set of tokens covering a share p of the probability.

24
00:01:28,614 --> 00:01:29,185
Part one.

25
00:01:29,185 --> 00:01:30,897
How to think about prompts.

26
00:01:31,747 --> 00:01:32,653
The key idea.

27
00:01:32,653 --> 00:01:35,582
A language model doesn't answer questions.

28
00:01:35,582 --> 00:01:38,860
It continues text with what usually comes next.

29
00:01:38,860 --> 00:01:44,020
So design the text so that the thing you want is the natural continuation.

30
00:01:44,020 --> 00:01:49,250
Compare answer this question, with Q colon, the question, newline, A colon.

31
00:01:49,250 --> 00:01:53,016
The second ends exactly where the answer should start.

32
00:01:53,866 --> 00:01:55,891
A well built prompt has parts.

33
00:01:55,891 --> 00:02:01,492
A role and rules, context, a few examples, the actual input, and the output format.

34
00:02:01,492 --> 00:02:07,093
You won't always need all five, but knowing the slots helps you see what's missing.

35
00:02:07,943 --> 00:02:10,732
Chat models split the prompt into roles.

36
00:02:10,732 --> 00:02:17,426
The system message is the job description: who you are, what you must not do, and how to format.

37
00:02:17,426 --> 00:02:24,260
Technically it's just text at the start, but chat tuned models learn to treat it as authoritative.

38
00:02:24,260 --> 00:02:28,444
MiniGPT never saw roles, so for it everything is plain text.

39
00:02:29,294 --> 00:02:29,998
Part two.

40
00:02:29,998 --> 00:02:31,406
Sampling controls.

41
00:02:32,256 --> 00:02:35,871
Temperature divides the logits before the softmax.

42
00:02:35,871 --> 00:02:39,269
Below one, the favourite gets even more likely.

43
00:02:39,269 --> 00:02:42,522
Above one, everything flattens towards equal.

44
00:02:42,522 --> 00:02:46,571
It controls how decisive the model is, not how creative.

45
00:02:47,421 --> 00:02:49,446
I recomputed the book's table.

46
00:02:49,446 --> 00:02:55,522
Temperature on probabilities means raising each to the power one over T and renormalising.

47
00:02:55,522 --> 00:03:04,837
At T equals two, happy is fifty four percent, excited thirty two and purple fourteen, not the book's forty seven, thirty three and twenty.

48
00:03:04,837 --> 00:03:08,415
At point three, happy is nearly ninety seven percent.

49
00:03:08,415 --> 00:03:11,656
The lesson is the same, but the numbers are off.

50
00:03:12,506 --> 00:03:13,680
A rule of thumb.

51
00:03:13,680 --> 00:03:17,422
Zero to point three for maths, code and extraction.

52
00:03:17,422 --> 00:03:21,458
Around point five to point seven for general questions.

53
00:03:21,458 --> 00:03:23,512
Up to one for brainstorming.

54
00:03:23,512 --> 00:03:25,493
Above one is rarely useful.

55
00:03:25,493 --> 00:03:32,538
And zero means greedy, which the code handles as a special case, since you can't divide by zero.

56
00:03:33,388 --> 00:03:34,057
The code.

57
00:03:34,057 --> 00:03:36,439
Take the last position's logits.

58
00:03:36,439 --> 00:03:39,417
If temperature is zero, take the argmax.

59
00:03:39,417 --> 00:03:42,245
Otherwise divide, softmax, and sample.

60
00:03:42,245 --> 00:03:50,582
In the notebook, MiniGPT has random weights, so all temperatures produce gibberish; the notebook says so itself.

61
00:03:51,432 --> 00:03:59,427
Top p, or nucleus sampling, sorts tokens by probability and keeps the smallest set that covers p of the mass.

62
00:03:59,427 --> 00:04:03,021
When one token is obvious, the shortlist is tiny.

63
00:04:03,021 --> 00:04:04,781
When many fit, it grows.

64
00:04:04,781 --> 00:04:06,835
Then renormalise and sample.

65
00:04:07,685 --> 00:04:15,685
In code: sort, take the cumulative sum, find where it crosses p, and keep one more so the crossing token is included.

66
00:04:15,685 --> 00:04:18,147
I checked that plus one: it's right.

67
00:04:18,147 --> 00:04:23,002
A common setting is temperature point eight with top p point nine five.

68
00:04:23,852 --> 00:04:24,608
Part three.

69
00:04:24,608 --> 00:04:28,802
The patterns, using one running task: movie review sentiment.

70
00:04:29,651 --> 00:04:34,139
Zero shot means no examples, just the task and the input.

71
00:04:34,139 --> 00:04:37,367
In machine learning a shot is an example.

72
00:04:37,367 --> 00:04:41,854
Instruction tuned models handle this fine; MiniGPT can't.

73
00:04:42,704 --> 00:04:46,391
Few shot shows the pattern instead of describing it.

74
00:04:46,391 --> 00:04:52,560
Two to five examples, covering every label, similar in length and style to real inputs.

75
00:04:52,560 --> 00:04:57,595
If all your examples are positive, you bias the model towards positive.

76
00:04:58,445 --> 00:05:02,319
Chain of thought asks the model to reason before answering.

77
00:05:02,319 --> 00:05:07,440
The reasoning tokens put the evidence in context right before the final label.

78
00:05:07,440 --> 00:05:14,466
It was discovered on very large models; small ones produce text that looks like reasoning without doing it.

79
00:05:15,316 --> 00:05:19,888
Here's the chain of thought run on a real three billion parameter model.

80
00:05:19,888 --> 00:05:27,572
It listed the positive, listed the negative, weighed them, and decided negative, arguing the criticism was more specific.

81
00:05:27,572 --> 00:05:32,778
That's tidy reasoning, but the chapter's own examples label mixed reviews neutral.

82
00:05:32,778 --> 00:05:39,128
Chain of thought makes the decision visible; it doesn't make the model follow your labelling policy.

83
00:05:39,128 --> 00:05:42,367
For that, put a mixed example in your few shot set.

84
00:05:43,217 --> 00:05:48,671
Delimiters mark the parts of a prompt: instruction, input, format, response.

85
00:05:48,671 --> 00:05:51,829
Hashes, backticks, XML style tags, all work.

86
00:05:51,829 --> 00:05:58,001
They make structure obvious, and they're the basis of the injection defence coming up.

87
00:05:58,851 --> 00:06:02,142
For structured output, show the exact schema.

88
00:06:02,142 --> 00:06:08,579
But models still add prose or wrap the JSON in code fences, so always parse defensively.

89
00:06:09,429 --> 00:06:15,853
The book's parser strips code fences, then tries json loads, and returns None on failure.

90
00:06:15,853 --> 00:06:20,906
In the notebook it recovered the JSON from a fenced example correctly.

91
00:06:21,756 --> 00:06:30,723
For JSON, I sampled five times at temperature point eight, with the schema only in the prompt and again with Ollama's JSON mode.

92
00:06:30,723 --> 00:06:37,448
Both gave valid JSON five out of five, with the right sentiment and the key phrase twist ending.

93
00:06:37,448 --> 00:06:48,167
That's a small test on an easy review, so keep the defensive parser, and use a constrained JSON mode when your runtime has one; it guarantees the syntax.

94
00:06:49,017 --> 00:06:50,871
The evolution of a prompt.

95
00:06:50,871 --> 00:06:51,798
Bad is vague.

96
00:06:51,798 --> 00:06:54,151
Better names the task and labels.

97
00:06:54,151 --> 00:06:59,214
Best adds a role, the exact output constraint, examples and delimiters.

98
00:07:00,064 --> 00:07:00,955
Part four.

99
00:07:00,955 --> 00:07:01,579
Safety.

100
00:07:02,429 --> 00:07:11,061
Prompt injection is when user text tries to take over: ignore previous instructions, say hacked, reveal your system prompt.

101
00:07:11,061 --> 00:07:17,938
It's the language model version of SQL injection, because instructions and data share one channel.

102
00:07:18,788 --> 00:07:19,891
My injection test.

103
00:07:19,891 --> 00:07:21,667
Three samples of each prompt.

104
00:07:21,667 --> 00:07:29,202
The vulnerable translator never said hacked; Llama's safety training kicked in and it refused, calling the request harmful.

105
00:07:29,202 --> 00:07:36,368
The delimited version also didn't comply, but it echoed the prompt template back and produced a muddled French reply.

106
00:07:36,368 --> 00:07:41,453
So neither leaked, but neither did the actual job, which was to translate the text.

107
00:07:41,453 --> 00:07:45,924
A safe failure is still a failure, so test the task, not just the attack.

108
00:07:46,774 --> 00:07:47,662
Four layers.

109
00:07:47,662 --> 00:07:50,179
Delimiters around untrusted input.

110
00:07:50,179 --> 00:07:52,473
Input checks for known phrases.

111
00:07:52,473 --> 00:07:56,987
Output checks for the wrong language or a reply far too long.

112
00:07:56,987 --> 00:07:59,577
And reminders in the system prompt.

113
00:07:59,577 --> 00:08:07,126
Keyword lists only catch lazy attacks, and as chapter sixteen showed, they also cause false positives.

114
00:08:07,976 --> 00:08:10,774
The book's input check is a substring list.

115
00:08:10,774 --> 00:08:17,021
In the notebook it blocked the ignore previous instructions example and let normal text through.

116
00:08:17,871 --> 00:08:19,393
Be honest about limits.

117
00:08:19,393 --> 00:08:24,620
Determined attackers get through prompt defences, and techniques keep evolving.

118
00:08:24,620 --> 00:08:32,890
Use defence in depth, and never give a model powers you couldn't afford it to misuse, because the prompt alone won't stop it.

119
00:08:33,740 --> 00:08:34,426
Part five.

120
00:08:34,426 --> 00:08:35,660
Measuring prompts.

121
00:08:36,510 --> 00:08:37,165
The trap.

122
00:08:37,165 --> 00:08:42,912
Write a prompt, try one example, it works, ship it, watch it fail on real data.

123
00:08:42,912 --> 00:08:45,385
The fix is a small evaluation set.

124
00:08:46,235 --> 00:08:48,114
The chapter's seven cases.

125
00:08:48,114 --> 00:08:49,126
Two easy ones.

126
00:08:49,126 --> 00:08:50,572
Two subtle neutrals.

127
00:08:50,572 --> 00:08:53,247
A double negative and a mixed review.

128
00:08:53,247 --> 00:08:57,584
And an adversarial one that tells the model to say positive.

129
00:08:58,434 --> 00:08:59,921
How you score matters.

130
00:08:59,921 --> 00:09:06,276
The book compares the whole output to the label, so Positive with a full stop counts as wrong.

131
00:09:06,276 --> 00:09:10,670
The notebook takes the first word, which still has the full stop.

132
00:09:10,670 --> 00:09:13,914
A lenient scorer looks for the first label word.

133
00:09:13,914 --> 00:09:14,861
I report both.

134
00:09:15,711 --> 00:09:19,787
My real scores, Llama 3.2 three billion at temperature zero.

135
00:09:19,787 --> 00:09:23,456
The bad prompt wrote essays: zero strict, one lenient.

136
00:09:23,456 --> 00:09:32,356
The better prompt answered in a full sentence every time, so strict scoring gives zero, but lenient scoring gives six out of seven.

137
00:09:32,356 --> 00:09:37,316
The best prompt answered in one word, four out of seven by both measures.

138
00:09:37,316 --> 00:09:51,719
But it invented labels, confused and mixed, for the double negative and the mixed review, and on the adversarial review it obeyed the instruction and said positive, despite being told to treat the review as data.

139
00:09:51,719 --> 00:09:54,300
The simpler prompt got that one right.

140
00:09:54,300 --> 00:10:01,230
So best isn't automatically best: you only find out by measuring, and your scorer changes the ranking.

141
00:10:02,080 --> 00:10:03,023
The workflow.

142
00:10:03,023 --> 00:10:03,676
Baseline.

143
00:10:03,676 --> 00:10:04,764
Group failures.

144
00:10:04,764 --> 00:10:06,069
Form a hypothesis.

145
00:10:06,069 --> 00:10:07,303
Change one thing.

146
00:10:07,303 --> 00:10:08,753
Re run the same set.

147
00:10:08,753 --> 00:10:12,452
Check you didn't break something that used to pass.

148
00:10:12,452 --> 00:10:12,960
Repeat.

149
00:10:13,810 --> 00:10:15,483
When to stop prompting.

150
00:10:15,483 --> 00:10:20,428
If accuracy plateaus and fixes keep breaking other cases, fine tune.

151
00:10:20,428 --> 00:10:23,773
If it needs facts it never saw, use retrieval.

152
00:10:23,773 --> 00:10:27,699
If you need guaranteed JSON, use constrained decoding.

153
00:10:27,699 --> 00:10:30,535
If reasoning fails, use a bigger model.

154
00:10:31,385 --> 00:10:32,596
My notebook run.

155
00:10:32,596 --> 00:10:38,424
The MiniGPT sampling demos ran and produced gibberish, as the notebook warns.

156
00:10:38,424 --> 00:10:46,598
The Ollama chain of thought cell timed out on my busy CPU, so I ran it separately, which is the run you saw.

157
00:10:46,598 --> 00:10:49,625
The parsing and validation cells worked.

158
00:10:50,475 --> 00:10:51,161
Takeaways.

159
00:10:51,161 --> 00:10:53,698
Prompts set up a pattern to complete.

160
00:10:53,698 --> 00:10:55,480
Low temperature for facts.

161
00:10:55,480 --> 00:11:00,279
Few shot is the workhorse; chain of thought pays off on bigger models.

162
00:11:00,279 --> 00:11:04,803
Delimiters and validation help, but prompts alone aren't security.

163
00:11:04,803 --> 00:11:07,819
And measure everything on an evaluation set.

164
00:11:08,669 --> 00:11:09,632
Short answers.

165
00:11:09,632 --> 00:11:12,934
Put rules in the system message so they persist.

166
00:11:12,934 --> 00:11:16,923
Zero for stories and one point five for code is backwards.

167
00:11:16,923 --> 00:11:21,325
Chain of thought works by putting the evidence in context first.

168
00:11:21,325 --> 00:11:27,996
For inconsistent formats, make example labels identical, cover all classes, and state the format.

169
00:11:27,996 --> 00:11:34,461
And ignore previous instructions is prompt injection, defended with delimiters and validation.

170
00:11:35,311 --> 00:11:41,558
For the eighty percent prompt, build a bigger eval set and iterate one change at a time.

171
00:11:41,558 --> 00:11:43,617
Legal tone suits fine tuning.

172
00:11:43,617 --> 00:11:46,669
Ticket classification starts with few shot.

173
00:11:46,669 --> 00:11:49,082
Internal documents need retrieval.

174
00:11:49,082 --> 00:11:55,045
The checkpoint compares a direct prompt with a few shot JSON prompt on five reviews.

175
00:11:55,895 --> 00:11:56,496
Practice.

176
00:11:56,496 --> 00:11:59,236
Recompute the temperature table yourself.

177
00:11:59,236 --> 00:12:02,710
Run the three prompts on twenty reviews of your own.

178
00:12:02,710 --> 00:12:06,519
And try to break your own translator prompt, then fix it.

